WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Beauty brands are talking a&nbsp;
lot more about sustainability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.340
and the environment and it makes sense

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:10.020
climate change is a huge problem and most&nbsp;
of us don't want to make things worse

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.460
well maybe not him

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.820
but beauty marketing is full of false&nbsp;
promises and meaningless buzzwords

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.780
what's actually better for the&nbsp;
environment and what is feel-good fluff?

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.000
well most of it is fluff

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.420
I'm Michelle chem PhD cosmetic&nbsp;
chemist and marketing connoisseur

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.520
I'm here with Jen another cosmetic chemist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.340
and science communicator who&nbsp;
specialises in sustainability

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.100
we're going to break down some&nbsp;
of the most common myths that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.400
brands use to pretend they're greener&nbsp;
than they are so you can see through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.060
the marketing more easily and make&nbsp;
informed decisions about what to buy

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.320
but it's not all doom and gloom we will&nbsp;
also go through things we can do to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.500
actually be more sustainable&nbsp;
so stick around till the end

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.460
let's start with the idea that natural&nbsp;
ingredients are better for the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.600
than synthetic ones when you finish using&nbsp;
them and they drift off into the environment

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.360
this myth shows up a lot with sunscreens

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.680
some places are banning chemical sunscreens&nbsp;
because of the idea that they harm coral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.280
reefs but this isn't based on good&nbsp;
evidence I've talked about it before

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.900
instead these places are promoting mineral&nbsp;
sunscreens mostly with zinc oxide based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.360
on on this assumption that it is natural and&nbsp;
therefore won't harm coral or any other wildlife

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.280
this is not true

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.320
when zinc oxide sunscreen gets washed off your&nbsp;
body it doesn't necessarily end up someplace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.660
that naturally has zinc oxide and even if it does&nbsp;
it can raise the concentration there compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:33.540
before and how harmful something is depends on how&nbsp;
much there is so extra zinc oxide can be a problem

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.820
there are actually a lot of studies showing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.000
zinc oxide is toxic to coral&nbsp;
and other aquatic organisms

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.180
for example these data tables come from a 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.720
National Academies report on the&nbsp;
environmental impacts of sunscreen

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.340
each of these rows is an example of a harmful&nbsp;
effect that zinc oxide has been found to have

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.360
there's also this diagram that compares the&nbsp;
toxicity of different chemical and physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.520
sunscreen ingredients each black dot is an&nbsp;
amount that caused a toxic effect in a study

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.600
the lower a dot is on this graph the less&nbsp;
you need of that ingredient to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.960
harmful effect - in other words&nbsp;
the more potently harmful it is

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.060
you can see that both zinc oxide and titanium&nbsp;
dioxide (the other "natural" sunscreen) can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.860
start causing harm at lower concentrations&nbsp;
than some "unnatural" chemical sunscreens

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.320
as the report says these two categories of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.220
UV filters completely overlap&nbsp;
with respect to acute toxicity

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.100
and this is still the case&nbsp;
with non-nano zinc oxide

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.860
nano refers to smaller particles that tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.920
be used in sunscreens because&nbsp;
they protect against UV better

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.440
and they look a bit less clown-like

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.960
in a lot of sunscreen marketing you'll see this&nbsp;
idea that if you use larger non-nano particles it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:45.360
will be completely safe but zinc oxide dissolves&nbsp;
in water which means larger particles get smaller

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.940
like and subscribe for more&nbsp;
mind-blowing facts like these

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.880
plus those dissolved zinc ions can also be harmful

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.760
zinc is a micronutrient that living organisms need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.020
but it is also a heavy metal -&nbsp;
it's toxic if there's too much

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.000
that's why if you look up the safety data sheet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
for zinc oxide you'll see this&nbsp;
symbol featuring a dead fish

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.720
it's surrounded by warnings about&nbsp;
how it's "very toxic to aquatic life"

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.480
zinc oxide even when in non-nano form has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.900
been recognised as potentially&nbsp;
toxic for a really long time

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.700
neither chemical nor physical sunscreen&nbsp;
seems to be a big issue for coral reefs

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:22.080
based on the relevant evidence so far the&nbsp;
amounts near reefs are too low to be having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.820
that much of an impact especially in light&nbsp;
of other stressors like rising temperatures

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.240
research is still ongoing also&nbsp;
for other aquatic organisms

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.800
but it's pretty clear that zinc oxide&nbsp;
isn't a completely safe alternative and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.900
this goes for pretty much any natural ingredient

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.560
just because it's natural doesn't&nbsp;
mean it's safe which you hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.320
already know because nature&nbsp;
is always trying to kill us

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.300
also the zinc oxide and titanium dioxide in&nbsp;
sunscreens isn't really natural most of the time

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.680
it's usually man-made

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.840
that's because if you just take them from nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.220
they're often contaminated with very&nbsp;
natural toxic heavy metals like lead

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.600
this is also the reason why natural iron&nbsp;
oxides which are the skin colored pigments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.560
in foundation aren't allowed in cosmetics&nbsp;
in the US they have to be synthetic

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.920
when we talk about the environmental&nbsp;
impacts of different ingredients in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.420
our products we usually focus on where the&nbsp;
ingredients go after we finish using them

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.500
this is going to be a recurring theme&nbsp;
what we can see and control is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.900
what we think about where the&nbsp;
product goes after we've used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.560
it (we can see it going down the drain)&nbsp;
and what we do with the empty packaging

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.400
but this is only one part of&nbsp;
environmental impact there's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.240
getting those ingredients in the first&nbsp;
place and a lot of people assume that

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.980
natural ingredients are more environmentally&nbsp;
friendly to produce than synthetic ones

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.580
again this is not true and this production part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.720
is a really big factor when it comes&nbsp;
to the overall environmental impact

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.880
the more holistic way of thinking about impact&nbsp;
is to look at the whole life cycle of the product

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.120
this is the life cycle most of the time we focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.900
on this part which is disposal and&nbsp;
recycling also called end of life

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.120
but there's a lot that happens before the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.020
actually gets to you and all of&nbsp;
these have environmental impacts

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.180
the raw materials get processed and turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.700
into ingredients which then&nbsp;
get turned into your product

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.800
the same steps happen to make the packaging

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.140
your product then gets shipped a bunch of times

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.180
there's also a bunch of&nbsp;
shipping in that first part

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.660
then it ends up at your house&nbsp;
you use it which is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.180
big part of the impact for example with water use

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.820
and THEN there's the disposal and recycling

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.080
we've already talked about the disposal of&nbsp;
ingredients and how natural isn't always better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.640
but natural also isn't automatically&nbsp;
better when it comes to production

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.327
when we hear natural it kind of sounds like

00:05:32.327 --> 00:05:36.360
[ASMR voice] someone wanders through a&nbsp;
forest and picks leaves off trees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.800
mashes them to make your products&nbsp;
while birds chirp and deer dance

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.020
it just SOUNDS more sustainable

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.840
but that is pretty far from reality

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.040
there are lots of natural ingredients that&nbsp;
have huge environmental costs to produce

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.300
for example essential oils it can take&nbsp;
hundreds to thousands of kilograms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:59.220
plant material to get a single kilogram of&nbsp;
essential oil and many essential oils are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.900
mostly sourced from plants growing in the wild&nbsp;
or at least the wild versions are more valuable

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.440
one example that Jen's talked&nbsp;
about before is Indian sandalwood

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.720
Jen: Sandalwood is the most economically&nbsp;
important tree globally the value hinges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.920
on aromatic chemical composition&nbsp;
namely high santalol content

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.580
santalol content is&nbsp;
concentrated as trees get older

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.940
harvesting generally happens&nbsp;
when the trees are 30 years old

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.240
this coupled with growing demand plus widespread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.180
fungal infection has driven&nbsp;
the species to endangerment

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.800
adulteration smuggling and illicit&nbsp;
trade remain major challenges

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.060
Michelle: another example is licorice&nbsp;
extract in skincare it's usually wild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:43.530
harvested licorice that's used because it has&nbsp;
a higher concentration of the active components

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:48.720
Jen: wild licorice has been uncontrollably&nbsp;
over harvested to the point that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.620
now more licorice being sold in the&nbsp;
market than there is being produced

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:58.200
licorice helps soils with sand management and&nbsp;
therefore over exploitation of wild licorice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.340
in the sandy regions it typically grows&nbsp;
directly contributes to desertification

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.700
Michelle: for both sandalwood and&nbsp;
licorice the obvious solution is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.180
to limit wild harvesting which is&nbsp;
harder than it sounds and switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.960
farmed versions which is a bit less&nbsp;
natural and usually lower quality

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.200
there's been efforts to increase&nbsp;
the quality of the farmed versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:20.580
but that is also harder than it sounds&nbsp;
and even though in these cases farming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.600
is more sustainable than wild harvesting&nbsp;
it is still not great for the environment

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.540
Jen: agriculture has a huge&nbsp;
impact on the environment

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:34.260
according to the 2019 IPBES report&nbsp;
agricultural expansion into intact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.740
ecosystems was the greatest&nbsp;
threat to biodiversity loss

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.880
agriculture is also a significant&nbsp;
contributor to greenhouse gas emissions

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.400
Michelle: and we don't just&nbsp;
farm cosmetic ingredients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.700
the land and resources could've&nbsp;
been used to produce food instead

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:52.020
Jen: food security is a really big&nbsp;
issue right now we have an immense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.860
global challenge of figuring out how&nbsp;
to feed our growing population with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.940
the limited resources we have&nbsp;
while also reducing our impacts

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.320
from my perspective it's hugely&nbsp;
problematic to divert agrispace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.240
away from food towards production of things&nbsp;
like cosmetics or aroma chemicals especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.480
if there's a less impactful way&nbsp;
of production already available

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.755
Michelle: and these less impactful ways&nbsp;
of production can be natural or synthetic

00:08:17.755 --> 00:08:20.280
Jen: ultimately it's complex and it depends

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:24.600
many natural materials in cosmetics&nbsp;
are byproducts of food agriculture or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:29.220
even biodiesel production albeit this in&nbsp;
itself is problematic for similar reasons

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:33.300
some essential oils are produced&nbsp;
via byproducts and can be considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:37.860
as upcycled for these the continued&nbsp;
use of natural materials makes sense

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:41.880
Michelle: and a lot of the time ingredients&nbsp;
made from byproducts are also cheaper

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.960
this links to another really annoying&nbsp;
marketing trope a lot of clean natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.920
brands like to brag about how precious and&nbsp;
rare their ingredients are how many rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.080
petals are in each bottle how long it took to&nbsp;
grow and they shame other ingredients which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.080 --> 00:09:00.300
usually synthetic for being "cheap" and then at&nbsp;
the same time they claim to be more sustainable

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:06.720
this makes zero sense if more resources went into&nbsp;
making a product that is not more sustainable

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.220
"cheaper" ingredients that are made from&nbsp;
salvaged waste materials are usually a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:14.940
sustainable option even though there&nbsp;
is this sort of snobbery against them

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:21.300
turning waste into something useful and valuable&nbsp;
is a good thing from an environmental perspective

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.160
and on this theme of making the most out&nbsp;
of limited resources here is the next myth

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.900
organically farmed ingredients are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.480
environmentally friendly than&nbsp;
conventionally farmed ones

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.560
most people think "organic" means more&nbsp;
environmentally friendly because that is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.060
organic farming organisations say that their goal&nbsp;
is it's in all four of their founding principles

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.080
but if you look at the actual rules that&nbsp;
farmers need to follow to be "certified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.440
organic" you'll see that they aren't&nbsp;
actually based on scientific evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:49.500
it's mostly just based on this idea of naturalness

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.380
"in general synthetic substances [like pesticides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:56.760
and fertilizers] are prohibited [...]&nbsp;
and non-synthetic substances are allowed"

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.520
but like we've discussed natural isn't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.400
better for the environment and&nbsp;
this applies to pesticides too

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:04.440
more and more studies have found that organic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.580
farming can actually end up with greater&nbsp;
environmental impacts in a lot of areas

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.140
this 2017 meta-analysis compared a bunch&nbsp;
of studies looking at the environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:18.000
impacts of conventional versus organic farming&nbsp;
they looked at producing 90 different foods

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.280
the higher a dot is on this graph the worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.740
organic farming is compared&nbsp;
to conventional farming

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.460
first off you can see that a lot of it&nbsp;
depends on the type of food the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:30.360
colored dots represent different categories&nbsp;
of food and they're at different heights

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:34.620
there is also a lot of variation for the&nbsp;
specific farms in different countries

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:37.200
for a lot of the data sets&nbsp;
there's a really long line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.540
which means that there is a huge range involved

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:44.040
so it isn't like organic is always better&nbsp;
or conventional is always better but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:48.480
are some categories of environmental&nbsp;
impact where organic is generally worse

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.460
and since then there's been more analyses where&nbsp;
scientists have started taking land use into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:58.560
account when looking at the greenhouse&nbsp;
gas impact which this analysis didn't do

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.540
organic farming tends to be less efficient&nbsp;
more land is needed to produce the same amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.040
of a crop and if you're using that land for&nbsp;
farming you will generally need to clear trees

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.340
having less trees means that you&nbsp;
have less carbon dioxide being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:15.060
absorbed so there is essentially&nbsp;
going to be an opportunity cost

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:18.600
and if you factor that greater land use in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.560
turns out that organic farming usually&nbsp;
produces a lot more greenhouse gases

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.600
one analysis found that in Sweden organic&nbsp;
peas would have a 50% greater greenhouse gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:31.980
impact than conventionally farmed peas for&nbsp;
winter wheat the difference was 70 percent

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:36.120
Jen: in order to meet our growing&nbsp;
demand in light of the environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:40.020
limitations and impacts associated&nbsp;
with agriculture we need to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.980
out how to produce more ingredients&nbsp;
with the same or even less impact

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.320
despite that there is a market push to tell&nbsp;
us that organic is sustainable there are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:54.120
serious limitations of organic agriculture&nbsp;
especially pertaining to its efficiency

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.880
this isn't to say that organic agriculture&nbsp;
is unsustainable full stop in some regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:05.220
it may make sense in the same way that in&nbsp;
other regions conventional agriculture or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.740
even genetic engineering may be the better option

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:12.600
ultimately there are no panaceas when it&nbsp;
comes to sustainability in agriculture

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.220
Michelle: whether organic or conventional methods&nbsp;
are better changes a lot depending on the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:21.420
and what impact you're looking at not even&nbsp;
just with the specific crops and environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:25.560
impacts but also things like pesticide&nbsp;
exposure for farmers and consumer prices

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:30.840
even if we look at biodiversity which is one of&nbsp;
the more widely accepted advantages of organic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:35.880
farming it turns out that if you take that extra&nbsp;
land use into account on average it breaks even

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:41.040
the obvious solution would be to use whatever&nbsp;
methods have lower environmental impacts in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:45.780
particular situation whether they're organic&nbsp;
or not but organic doesn't allow that it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:49.860
limits the options that farmers have based on&nbsp;
this misconception that "natural is better"

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:54.240
in some situations like the cereal&nbsp;
crops and bird biodiversity it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:57.360
better to produce the same amount&nbsp;
of food in a smaller area using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:01.620
conventional farming and leave a strip&nbsp;
of unfarmed land than it is to use a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:06.240
larger area for organic farming but that&nbsp;
would not be allowed under organic rules

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.420
and there are more examples now of&nbsp;
more environmentally sustainable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.240
farming practices that kind of just ignore organic

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.900
and we haven't actually dealt with that&nbsp;
mould thing yet natural products tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:19.860
have shorter shelf lives and go off faster&nbsp;
than products with more synthetic components

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.080
a lot of synthetic ingredients were originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.080
designed as improvements on&nbsp;
naturally occurring chemicals

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:29.280
it isn't always the case but there is&nbsp;
a reason why no one is that surprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:32.940
where natural products have texture or&nbsp;
color changes or start growing mould

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:36.900
and if you have to throw out half the product&nbsp;
that means that the stuff that you used with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:40.860
that waste taken into account will be a&nbsp;
lot more resource intensive to produce

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:44.040
speaking of natural things&nbsp;
decomposing our next myth

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:47.880
making synthetic ingredients from&nbsp;
petrochemicals is worse for the environment

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.420
there's this idea that synthetic&nbsp;
ingredients made from petroleum are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.660
bad for the environment because&nbsp;
petroleum is a fossil fuel

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:59.400
yes fossil fuels are bad for the environment&nbsp;
extracting petroleum through processes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:03.660
fracking can be really destructive and there's&nbsp;
also all the carbon dioxide that gets released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:07.920
when they're burned as a fuel to release energy&nbsp;
which is the main driver of climate change

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:12.060
but there are some big differences&nbsp;
between petroleum as a fuel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.590
ingredients that are made from petroleum

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:18.180
Jen: there is no petroleum sourcing with&nbsp;
the intent of making cosmetic ingredients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:22.980
the petrochemicals that are used are all&nbsp;
waste products from the petroleum sector

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:28.800
societally we want to move away from our&nbsp;
reliance of fossil fuels but until the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:34.920
does and while the world is fueled by them the&nbsp;
byproducts that the cosmetics use will be there

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:39.360
in the future this may not be the&nbsp;
case but this is the current reality

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:43.200
Michelle: and making natural&nbsp;
ingredients also uses up fossil fuels

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:49.080
tilling making fertiliser and pesticides moving&nbsp;
water for irrigation harvesting transporting plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:54.720
materials processing purification all of this&nbsp;
uses energy which usually comes from fossil fuels

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.800
and this time the fossil fuels ARE&nbsp;
purposely dug up and burned as fuels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:03.240
to get out energy and that burning is the part&nbsp;
that releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.780
if petroleum jelly isn't burnt&nbsp;
then that carbon stays sequestered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:10.320
in the petroleum jelly and it won't&nbsp;
directly contribute to climate change

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:15.180
again it depends on the specific situation&nbsp;
synthetic isn't always less impactful than natural

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:20.040
making synthetic ingredients processing&nbsp;
and purifying them also uses energy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.320
again this mostly comes from burning fossil fuels

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:25.920
and again some natural ingredients are&nbsp;
also made from waste materials from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.400
other processes which means energy&nbsp;
savings and less carbon dioxide

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.700
so it all comes back to looking&nbsp;
at the data for each individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:36.180
ingredient which means life&nbsp;
cycle analyses a few examples

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:41.040
Jen: an LCA from Symrise where they show&nbsp;
that their synthetically produced menthol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:47.280
produced 8 kilograms of CO2 per kilogram menthol&nbsp;
versus 50 to 100 for its natural counterpart

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:52.260
in contrast we can look to Genomatica's&nbsp;
Brontide butylene glycol where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:55.500
halved their CO2 emissions with&nbsp;
the use of plant-based starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.500 --> 00:16:00.780
points via biotechnology instead of the&nbsp;
traditional production from fossil fuels

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:05.220
then again since they use a genetically&nbsp;
modified biocatalyst in this process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:09.480
many consumers and ecocertifiers&nbsp;
still view this as unsustainable

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:14.940
there is no blanket rule about natural always&nbsp;
being more sustainable when we're considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:19.860
whether or not natural materials are more or&nbsp;
less impactful we should be asking ourselves

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:25.560
are we using prime agricultural land to&nbsp;
produce them or are they coming from waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:30.240
or byproducts from crops that we're&nbsp;
already using anyway in other sectors

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.660
Michelle: this also means there's no&nbsp;
blanket rule about what benefits the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:38.040
fossil fuel industry more buying synthetic&nbsp;
ingredients made from petrochemicals versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:41.400
buying natural ingredients that use&nbsp;
a lot of fossil fuels to produce

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:45.840
and speaking of myths about petrochemicals&nbsp;
we need to talk about packaging

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.440
the idea that plastic is always worse&nbsp;
for the environment than other types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:55.740
packaging is everywhere not just in cosmetics&nbsp;
and it is again a massive oversimplification

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.760
like I mentioned before a lot of this&nbsp;
is because we tend to focus on just that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:03.480
end of life part when we choose products&nbsp;
because we have to personally deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:07.740
it does the bottle go into the bin or can&nbsp;
it be reused or recycled does it degrade

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:11.820
and just like before end of life is&nbsp;
just one part of environmental impact

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:16.920
Jen: if we stop using all plastic and use&nbsp;
more "eco-friendly" alternatives instead like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:23.880
glass paper and aluminum we would need on&nbsp;
average 3.6 times the material 2.2 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:30.120
the energy and there would be an average increase&nbsp;
of about 2.7 times the carbon dioxide emissions

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:34.200
this makes a lot of sense if we look&nbsp;
at each part of the life cycle plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.080
isn't universally the worst option for any part

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:42.180
for example with production plastic is&nbsp;
produced via byproducts of the petroleum sector

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:48.180
contrast that to paper where there's a lot of&nbsp;
processing from wood and then glass and aluminum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:54.180
they can come from recycled sources but there is&nbsp;
a lot of heat involved that increases the impact

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:57.900
Michelle: one of the things we need&nbsp;
to remember is why plastic packaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:02.730
became so widespread in the first place it is&nbsp;
really good at protecting the stuff inside it

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:07.680
Jen: to transportation for example&nbsp;
with glass versus plastic plastic is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:12.780
lightweight for its strength and therefore the&nbsp;
transportation CO2 emissions tend to be less

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.480
there's also the matter of glass breakage

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:23.040
And similar for aluminum - aluminum is heavier it&nbsp;
dents easily and people don't want to buy it then

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:27.780
even for end of life mono material&nbsp;
plastic can be recycled about 40 times

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:33.540
and some companies to strive for eco options&nbsp;
are switching to partly paper packaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:39.000
which ends up needing a plastic layer since&nbsp;
paper doesn't stand up to for example water

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:42.660
and then as a result they end&nbsp;
up using multi-layered materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:45.420
that are harder to recycle and ends up being worse

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:50.820
ultimately here the best packaging option&nbsp;
in terms of the environmental impact is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:56.880
case-by-case basis with the best option varying&nbsp;
greatly from individual formulations and users

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:02.760
for example what are the user's habits what kind&nbsp;
of recycling infrastructure do they have available

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:07.320
is your bottle return program&nbsp;
something that they can viably access

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.020
blanket statements that plastic equals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:14.700
bad are both from my perspective&nbsp;
unhelpful and potentially harmful

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:18.540
at the end of the day there is&nbsp;
no silver bullet for sustainable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:21.480
packaging and every option has its pros and cons

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:24.840
Michelle: so hopefully from all of&nbsp;
this it's clear that sustainability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:28.560
is complicated and straightforward&nbsp;
blanket rules just don't work

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.740
we can't just always choose natural&nbsp;
or organic products or choose glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:34.980
over plastic and expect that&nbsp;
we are making the better choice

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:37.740
so what can we actually do to be more sustainable

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:42.960
i think the most obvious thing to do is to just&nbsp;
consume less buying something is almost always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:47.310
going to be less sustainable than not buying&nbsp;
something regardless of whether it's eco-friendly

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:51.900
Jen: per capita consumerism is the most&nbsp;
environmentally destructive thing we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:55.860
societally the overwhelming evidence shows&nbsp;
that the negative impacts associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.860 --> 00:20:00.900
over consumption vastly outweigh any of the&nbsp;
positives associated with "green" innovation

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:03.420
Michelle: so it's important to&nbsp;
consider if you really need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:07.740
buy something - because a lot of companies make&nbsp;
it seem like you'll be saving the planet just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:12.600
by buying their product when in reality it is&nbsp;
probably going to have a net negative impact

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:16.680
and this is filtering into regulation&nbsp;
for example in the uk they've already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.700
said that labels like green sustainable&nbsp;
and eco-friendly could be considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:25.380
deceptive unless a business can&nbsp;
prove that as a whole there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.950
positive environmental impact to using&nbsp;
the product which is pretty unlikely

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:32.400
Jen: there's a push towards green&nbsp;
consumerism which is ultimately at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:37.740
odds with sustainability as it paradoxically&nbsp;
increases consumption which increases impact

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:41.580
also there is a market incentivization for green

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:46.740
consumers are willing to pay more for products&nbsp;
they perceive to be green and there is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:51.780
a rapidly growing market for impact or&nbsp;
ESG investment where investors want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:56.730
invest in companies they again perceive&nbsp;
to be socially or ecologically superior

00:20:56.730 --> 00:21:01.140
Michelle: given how how complicated and&nbsp;
context dependent sustainability is I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.780
think it is also really important to ask questions

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.680
if a brand says that their product&nbsp;
is sustainable ask them for evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.140
because there's so much complexity we can't work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:13.680
out what's going on without brands&nbsp;
providing some level of transparency

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:17.220
we don't know how the butylene glycol&nbsp;
in their product was made we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.560
know whether their production&nbsp;
plant uses renewable energy

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:23.580
Jen: companies pursuing sustainable&nbsp;
development must have access to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:27.660
much relevant information as they can in&nbsp;
order to make the best possible decision

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:33.120
to get this information they must measure and&nbsp;
record data in terms of their specific goals

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:37.260
environmental accounting is the&nbsp;
backbone of sustainable development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:41.160
because you can't be accountable unless&nbsp;
you're actually accounting for things

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:46.260
life cycle assessments can be seen as the most&nbsp;
important tool for understanding a product's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:51.180
impact for example actually calculating&nbsp;
specific environmental impacts of every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:57.360
single ingredient and how much is going into the&nbsp;
product or how things are being transported etc

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:00.660
a lot of the time the clean movement just bases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:05.100
their ideologies on assumptions like&nbsp;
assuming that natural is always safer

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:11.100
if we want to move to reduce our impacts we&nbsp;
need substantiation and we need the specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:16.380
measures to be relevant and standardised so you&nbsp;
can actually make comparisons between companies

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:21.120
most of the current popular&nbsp;
"eco" standards certifiers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.640
the labels you see around don't do this very well

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:28.860
and as a consequence they may actually be&nbsp;
resulting in more greenwashing rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:32.640
true sustainability progress despite&nbsp;
how they're positioning themselves

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.860
Michelle: it's also important as consumers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:37.440
be informed about what things&nbsp;
are actually more sustainable

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:40.920
the beauty industry responds to&nbsp;
consumer demand if we push for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:46.500
the wrong thing like non-evidence based&nbsp;
eco-certifications or less plastic or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:50.040
natural ingredients regardless of what&nbsp;
the environmental impacts actually are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:54.420
brands are going to deliver it so we could&nbsp;
be contributing to making everything worse

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:58.620
like we saw with clean beauty people pushing&nbsp;
for paraben-free products meant that brands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:02.820
started using less effective and more&nbsp;
irritating allergenic preservatives that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:07.260
led to more skin reactions maybe an allergy&nbsp;
epidemic and products that go off faster

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:10.800
so it's important not to go for easy&nbsp;
answers and make sure you're sharing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:14.880
the correct information and if you see&nbsp;
people and brands spreading myths it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:18.180
important to counteract that and tell people&nbsp;
where they can find better information

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:23.580
Jen: a problem we're increasingly seeing is&nbsp;
misplaced conservation where misinformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:27.960
and polarization can actually work&nbsp;
together against sustainable development

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:33.360
for example by leading to a misallocation of&nbsp;
resources into efforts that fundamentally don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:37.920
make sense sometimes making the issues&nbsp;
that they're trying to address worse

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:43.200
a second way sustainability misinformation&nbsp;
is working against sustainable development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:49.380
is via green nudging when ineffective but&nbsp;
perhaps easier policy comes before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:55.380
effective but harder policy the effective&nbsp;
policy is less likely to be successful

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:59.460
if the effective policy comes first&nbsp;
the chances of success are much greater

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:03.300
one example here could be carbon&nbsp;
pricing well supported by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:06.720
scientific evidence to be an effective&nbsp;
strategy for climate change mitigation

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:11.160
having policy that focuses on things like&nbsp;
carbon footprinting which is widely debated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:16.260
as an effective strategy come before as&nbsp;
we're currently doing when we finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:19.800
get to the carbon pricing it's going&nbsp;
to be an increasingly uphill battle

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:26.100
the order of policy in environmental action in&nbsp;
general matters therefore relying on the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:29.820
available evidence is crucially important&nbsp;
as we pursue sustainable development

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:35.040
one final thing I want to add is that&nbsp;
if we want to make significant strides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:39.660
towards sustainability what we really&nbsp;
need are science-based policy changes

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:43.920
consumer scapegoating and corporate&nbsp;
sustainability aren't working

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:48.780
we've seen this with carbon pricing&nbsp;
which as mentioned is one of the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:52.920
well-supported policies for addressing&nbsp;
climate change but instead of focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:58.680
on implementing it effectively there's a lot&nbsp;
of blame on consumers and a lot of focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:03.600
things like carbon footprinting which has&nbsp;
incentivized a pretty problematic market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:08.280
for carbon offsets which is yet another&nbsp;
whole complex topic I won't get into here

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:13.080
Michelle: sustainability is obviously a huge topic&nbsp;
so we only covered a tiny fraction of it here

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.900
let us know what other questions&nbsp;
about sustainability you have

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.480
if you want to find out more about&nbsp;
these topics I've linked some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:21.480
Jen's posts and podcasts which are&nbsp;
amazing definitely check those out

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:24.780
we've also started Beauty SciComm which&nbsp;
will hopefully spread more accurate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.880
beauty science information check us out as well

